  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 309 AM EDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nAs we move into the later half of the weekend and into early next \nweek, the overall forecast thinking has not changed substantially. \nThe flow pattern across North America will generally be zonal with a \nslight ridge over the western CONUS.  The storm track will be well \nto our north with the strongest belt of westerlies running from the \nnorthern High Plains through the Great Lakes and into southern New \nEngland.  A perturbation within the mean flow will move eastward \nacross the Midwest and into the Great Lakes.  A cluster of strong to \nsevere storms looks to move through the Midwest and into the \nsouthern Great Lakes.  At this time, much of this activity looks to \nstay well north of the LMK CWA.  However, there is a slight chance \nthat some southward moving outflow could clip our far northeast \nareas and spark some showers/storms.  Latest LREF members keep PoPs \nacross the region very low (10-20%).  The main weather story will be \nan increasingly warm airmass developing across the region with highs \nSunday afternoon warming into the lower 90s.  Given that dewpoints \nwill be the low-mid 70s, it will be rather humid with afternoon heat \nindices warming into the mid-upper 90s.  Lows Sunday night will be \nin the 70-75 degree range. Similar conditions are expected on Monday \nwith afternoon highs in the lower 90s with afternoon heat indices \ntopping out in the 95-100 degree range.\n\nBy Tuesday, we'll see the upper level pattern amplify slightly with \nridging building across the western CONUS and downstream troughing \ndeveloping along the eastern US coast.  Locally, we'll see heights \nfall slightly with the flow aloft becoming more northwesterly with \ntime.  Most of the day on Tuesday looks to remain dry.  However, \nsmall scale perturbations will likely cruise through in the mean \nflow aloft and may bring a cluster of showers/storms to the region \nby late Tuesday.  Highs Tuesday afternoon will be in the lower 90s \nwith overnight lows in the 70-75 degree range.\n\nFor the remainder of the workweek, the flow aloft will remain \nnorthwesterly.  We'll see a few perturbations within the flow move \nthrough the region and these could bring episodic bouts of \nthunderstorms to the region.  Given model soundings and a slight \nincrease in wind shear, strong to severe storms could be possible at \ntimes along with heavy rainfall.  It is still way too early to \nspeculate on storm severity and locations at this time range. \nAdditionally, there continues to be some model spread on just where \nthe storm track will set up.  Recent model runs suggests that some \nstronger ridging may develop across TX and into the southeast which \nmay result in a slight northward shift of the storm track.  This \ncould lead to the storm corridor setting up from the Midwest down \nthrough the I-70 corridor.  The multiple rounds of convection will \nlikely lead to excessive rainfall from the Midwest into the \nmiddle/northern portions of the Ohio Valley.  Temperatures are \nexpected to be a little cooler with the expected shower/storms \naround.  Highs Wednesday through Friday look to warm into the upper \n80s with lows in the upper 60s/lower 70s.",
